{
  "id": "payments",
  "title": "Payments",
  "summary": "Idempotency keys, a ledger and an outbox for effectively-exactly-once payments.",
  "lesson": {
    "problem": "The network drops the response to \"charge €50\". The client retries. Did the customer just pay twice?",
    "why": [
      "Every payment request carries an idempotency key chosen by the client. The payment service stores the key with the result in the same transaction; a retry with the same key gets the stored result instead of a second charge.",
      "Exactly-once delivery is impossible over an unreliable network. What you can build is at-least-once delivery plus idempotent handling, which behaves like exactly-once.",
      "Updating the database and publishing an event are two writes that can half-fail. The transactional outbox writes the event into the database with the payment; a relay publishes it afterwards, retrying until it succeeds."
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "Make every payment operation idempotent and safe to retry.",
      "Record money movements in an append-only, double-entry ledger.",
      "Reconcile against the payment provider regularly: trust, but verify."
    ]
  },
